Found while checking the CLAUDE.md roadmap against the code.

App::karr::Task has depends_on (Task.pm:51), it is part of the field list
(:83), and to_markdown writes it into the frontmatter when non-empty (:335).
So a caller can set it, karr stores it in the task ref, and `karr materialize`
puts it in the file view where kanban-md would read it.

Nothing reads it. Verified: depends_on appears nowhere in lib/ outside those
three lines in Task.pm, and in no command at all -- not pick, not move, not
show, not board, not list.

The consequence is worse than a missing feature. A missing feature is visible;
this one looks present. An agent that records `depends_on: [5]` on a card has
every reason to believe karr will hold that card back until 5 is done, because
the field was accepted, stored and round-tripped. `karr pick` hands it out
regardless. In a multi-agent setup -- which is what this whole distribution is
for -- that means work starting in the wrong order with no warning anywhere.

Roadmap item 2 in CLAUDE.md ("dependency checking -- block tasks with
unsatisfied deps from being picked") is this. The data model half is already
built; what is missing is the evaluation.

Smallest honest step, if the full feature is not wanted now: make `karr show`
display depends_on, so at least the field is visible where a human looks. That
does not fix the silence in pick, but it stops the field from being invisible
as well as inert.

For the real fix, decisions needed rather than assumed: does an unsatisfied
dependency make a task unpickable, or only warn? What counts as satisfied --
any terminal status, or specifically done? What happens to a dependency on an
id that does not exist, or on an archived task? Does `karr move` into
in-progress get the same check as pick, or only pick? kanban-md's behaviour is
the reference for the defaults.
